#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363013 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3758cf02-ba4b-539a-bc61-8c2030ff6f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Exponential Equation by Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09d7897f-e3b2-57b2-b7b1-01f92a0ec301" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363015} {:answer/id #uuid "f1ab6678-a097-55ca-93a7-3ef7b79398f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363016}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09d7897f-e3b2-57b2-b7b1-01f92a0ec301" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363015} :answer-field/id #uuid "24f5bd3d-59cf-5b72-8150-e5c8201e3656" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363014} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f7dc122-e41f-574f-988b-3efd7f0e9375" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363018}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f7dc122-e41f-574f-988b-3efd7f0e9375" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363018} :answer-field/id #uuid "db1c35cb-79cc-5757-b226-76241242d528" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363017}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0d9c49d1-f2b1-5d75-aff3-0ef3f3409f78" :question/math-academy-id "q-237946" :question/problem "In **ascending order,** the solutions of the equation ${x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{3x}-9\\cdot {5}^{3x}=0$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{3x}-9\\cdot {5}^{3x} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-9)\\cdot {5}^{3x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-9=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{5}^{3x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}-9=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(x+3)(x-3)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-3,3.$\n\n- The equation ${5}^{3x}=0$ has no solutions because ${5}^{3x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the original equation has the solutions \n\n$$\nx=-3\\,\\text{and}\\,x=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363019 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3758cf02-ba4b-539a-bc61-8c2030ff6f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Exponential Equation by Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eda9e078-1943-5ebd-935c-afdc99ead1b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363021}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eda9e078-1943-5ebd-935c-afdc99ead1b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363021} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e926dec-382f-56be-b32f-b0ebe932a898" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363020} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf560934-9e52-58f3-a07f-85136b73967e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363023}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf560934-9e52-58f3-a07f-85136b73967e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363023} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec25c2f2-d84e-512e-b94a-6d9fdc97a5b6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363022}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7122b6e0-4bf0-565b-88a8-baed162e5764" :question/math-academy-id "q-264778" :question/problem "In **ascending order,** the solutions of the equation $2{x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{x+3}-7x\\cdot {2}^{x+3}+3\\cdot {2}^{x+3}=0$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{x+3}-7x\\cdot {2}^{x+3}+3\\cdot {2}^{x+3} & =0 \\\\ (2{x}^{2}-7x+3){2}^{x+3} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}-7x+3=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{2}^{x+3}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $2{x}^{2}-7x+3=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(2x-1)(x-3)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=\\frac{1}{2},3.$\n\n- The equation ${2}^{x+3}=0$ has no solutions because ${2}^{x+3}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the original equation has the solutions \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{and}\\,x=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363024 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8aaf12cf-8596-5960-bc68-5ca3eb5fab0f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Exponential Equation by Rearranging the Equation and Then Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a47b2ec-48db-586c-83c7-6986d749de3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363026} {:answer/id #uuid "3e596de6-42b8-5a22-a832-311c3c37b97a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363027}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a47b2ec-48db-586c-83c7-6986d749de3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363026} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b4874eb-da20-5fbe-98e8-a7d3cc21e86a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363025} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "934c528d-b0cd-55f4-ac56-4393ebb6b5a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363029}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "934c528d-b0cd-55f4-ac56-4393ebb6b5a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363029} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c64c6ea-e067-59f6-8d72-bd26b1e98cdc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363028}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a3bb3ced-7db7-5b87-94c1-62537aeec042" :question/math-academy-id "q-264786" :question/problem "In **ascending order,** the solutions of the equation $2{x}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{2x-5}=50\\cdot {3}^{2x-5}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{2x-5} & =50\\cdot {3}^{2x-5} \\\\ 2{x}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{2x-5}-50\\cdot {3}^{2x-5} & =0 \\\\ (2{x}^{2}-50)\\cdot {3}^{2x-5} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}-50=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{3}^{2x-5}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $2{x}^{2}-50=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}^{2}-25) & =0 \\\\ 2(x+5)(x-5) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-5,5.$\n\n- The equation $2=0$ is not a possible solution because it's a false statement.\n\n- The equation ${3}^{2x-5}=0$ has no solutions because ${3}^{2x-5}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the original equation has the solutions \n\n$$\nx=-5\\,\\text{and}\\,x=5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363030 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8aaf12cf-8596-5960-bc68-5ca3eb5fab0f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Exponential Equation by Rearranging the Equation and Then Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df3889dc-037c-57b4-b1e8-6f1ded27af82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363032}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df3889dc-037c-57b4-b1e8-6f1ded27af82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363032} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee2c1396-2e55-5c8d-8ebf-daab6e87b537" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363031} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e94f7cbc-7ffa-58a9-94e7-e5b531bc989c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363034}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e94f7cbc-7ffa-58a9-94e7-e5b531bc989c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363034} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cc6704d-aa5b-5127-87b8-aea648b10e80" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363033}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b6c4f0ee-1545-58ee-a3be-5f0c616e95a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-264793" :question/problem "In **ascending order,** the solutions of the equation $4{x}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{2x}+6\\cdot {e}^{2x}=25x\\cdot {e}^{2x}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{2x}+6\\cdot {e}^{2x} & =25x\\cdot {e}^{2x} \\\\ 4{x}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{2x}-25x\\cdot {e}^{2x}+6\\cdot {e}^{2x} & =0 \\\\ (4{x}^{2}-25x+6)\\cdot {e}^{2x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}-25x+6=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{e}^{2x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $4{x}^{2}-25x+6=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(4x-1)(x-6)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=\\frac{1}{4},x=6.$\n\n- The equation ${e}^{2x}=0$ has no solutions because ${e}^{2x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the original equation has the solutions \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{and}\\,x=6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363035 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3758cf02-ba4b-539a-bc61-8c2030ff6f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Exponential Equation by Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac127ffb-efe4-528d-8920-7336e28a9610" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363037} {:answer/id #uuid "0a2f68ec-59c1-55fe-9e7a-cefd24008237" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363038} {:answer/id #uuid "2d64f196-9115-505f-b0ca-5d59f8134ea5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363039} {:answer/id #uuid "cd145944-dc1c-507e-94ae-e20a971a4226" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363040} {:answer/id #uuid "9d2b57e2-2e1a-5b81-9f13-673d579e972e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363041}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a2f68ec-59c1-55fe-9e7a-cefd24008237" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363038} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa4e42d5-1b09-5857-9502-e6caf5ca0333" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363036}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1e70f367-6480-5897-b934-951d91fc00be" :question/math-academy-id "q-88460" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation $2{x}^{2}\\cdot {6}^{x+3}-9x\\cdot {6}^{x+3}+4\\cdot {6}^{x+3}=0?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}\\cdot {6}^{x+3}-9x\\cdot {6}^{x+3}+4\\cdot {6}^{x+3} & =0 \\\\ (2{x}^{2}-9x+4){6}^{x+3} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}-9x+4=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{6}^{x+3}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $2{x}^{2}-9x+4=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(2x-1)(x-4)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=\\frac{1}{2},4.$\n\n- The equation ${6}^{x+3}=0$ has no solutions because ${6}^{x+3}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=\\frac{1}{2},4,$ and their product is $\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4=2.$"}]}
